-- Q287: Internal Controls [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls implemented by the service organization at the user entity''s location","Controls the user entity must maintain for the service organization''s controls to function effectively","Optional controls the service auditor recommends for improved processing","Controls the PCAOB tests during its routine inspection of the service organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 402.09 and .A13, complementary user entity controls (CUECs) are controls the service organization''s system assumes the user entity will implement. AU-C 402.15 requires the user auditor to evaluate and test CUECs because the service organization''s controls alone may not achieve objectives without them.
Wrong (A): CUECs are implemented by the user entity, not by the service organization at the user entity''s location.
Wrong (C): CUECs are not optional recommendations — they are assumed to be in place by the service organization''s control design.
Wrong (D): PCAOB inspections focus on audit firm quality, not on testing CUECs at user entities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 287;

-- Q289: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both the revenue cycle and the expense cycle for the same period","Authorization, recording, and custody of assets for the same transaction","Both the external audit engagement and the annual tax return","Preparation of financial statements and the annual operating budget"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Segregation of duties requires separating three functions: authorization of transactions, recording (bookkeeping), and custody of related assets. When one person controls all three, the risk of undetected errors or fraud increases significantly per AU-C 315.A73.
Wrong (A): Revenue and expense accounts are different cycles, not the three incompatible functions that segregation of duties addresses.
Wrong (C): Audit and tax engagements are performed by external parties and are unrelated to internal segregation of duties.
Wrong (D): Preparing financial statements and budgets are both recording functions and do not create the incompatible-duties conflict.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 289;

-- Q290: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Covers controls for security and availability rather than financial reporting objectives","Reports on design and implementation of controls at a point in time, not operating effectiveness","Is intended for unrestricted general public distribution and use","Can only be issued by the PCAOB rather than an independent service auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 402.14, a SOC 1 Type 1 report covers the description and design of controls at a specific point in time. A Type 2 report goes further by also testing operating effectiveness over a specified period, typically six to twelve months.
Wrong (A): Both SOC 1 Type 1 and Type 2 reports address controls relevant to user entities'' financial reporting, not security and availability — those are SOC 2 criteria.
Wrong (C): SOC 1 reports are restricted-use reports intended for user entities and their auditors, not for general public distribution.
Wrong (D): SOC 1 reports are issued by independent service auditors under SSAE 18, not by the PCAOB.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 290;

-- Q291: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Modify the client''s accounting software to correct identified processing errors","Automatically generate and issue the final audit opinion on financial statements","Replace the need for substantive testing procedures on account balances","Read, analyze, and sample client data files without altering the original records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A82, generalized audit software (GAS) allows the auditor to directly access and analyze electronic data files. GAS capabilities include sorting, summarizing, selecting samples, comparing files, and detecting duplicates — all without modifying original data.
Wrong (A): GAS does not modify the client''s software or correct errors — it is a read-only analytical tool used by auditors.
Wrong (B): GAS analyzes data to support audit procedures but does not generate or issue the audit opinion itself.
Wrong (C): GAS supplements substantive procedures by enabling efficient data analysis but does not replace the need for them.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 291;

-- Q292: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only to the engagement quality reviewer on the audit team","To the SEC within 30 days of the finding''s identification","Orally to management during the scheduled exit conference","In writing to management and those charged with governance within 60 days"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 265.13, when a material weakness is identified during a non-issuer audit, the auditor must communicate it in writing to management and those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date.
Wrong (A): Communication must reach management and those charged with governance, not merely the engagement quality reviewer on the audit team.
Wrong (B): Non-issuer audits are governed by AU-C 265, not SEC reporting requirements — there is no SEC filing obligation for non-issuers.
Wrong (C): Oral communication alone is insufficient for material weaknesses; AU-C 265 specifically requires written communication.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 292;

-- Q294: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether account balances agree with amounts posted to the general ledger","Whether account balances are confirmed as accurate by independent third parties","Whether all recorded transactions have been properly authorized by management","Whether leading digits in financial data follow the expected mathematical distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Benford''s Law predicts the expected frequency distribution of leading digits in naturally occurring numerical data. Under AU-C 520, significant deviations from this expected pattern may indicate manipulation, errors, or fabricated data warranting further investigation.
Wrong (A): Reconciling balances to the general ledger is a standard substantive procedure, not an application of Benford''s Law digital analysis.
Wrong (B): Confirming balances with third parties is a confirmation procedure under AU-C 505, unrelated to digit distribution analysis.
Wrong (C): Testing transaction authorization is a controls test, not a digital analysis technique based on mathematical patterns.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 294;

-- Q302: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Individual account balances are large and the assessed risk of misstatement is high","The risk of misstatement is low, balances are small, and few exceptions are expected","Management has specifically refused to permit the use of positive confirmations","The auditor wants to obtain the highest possible level of audit assurance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 505.16, negative confirmations are appropriate when the risk of material misstatement is assessed as low, the population consists of many small balances, and the auditor expects few exceptions. Absence of a reply is treated as agreement.
Wrong (A): Large balances with high assessed risk warrant positive confirmations, which require explicit responses from recipients.
Wrong (C): Management''s refusal to allow positive confirmations does not make negative confirmations appropriate — it may constitute a scope limitation.
Wrong (D): Negative confirmations provide less assurance than positive confirmations because non-responses are assumed to agree.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 302;

-- Q304: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Increase the total number of individual items that must be tested","Reduce variability within each subgroup, potentially reducing required sample size","Eliminate all sampling risk from the auditor''s testing procedures","Ensure that only the largest dollar items in the population are tested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.A11, stratification divides the population into subgroups with similar characteristics, reducing variability within each stratum. This allows the auditor to design more efficient samples, often resulting in a smaller total sample size while maintaining desired assurance.
Wrong (A): Stratification typically reduces rather than increases the total number of items tested by improving sampling efficiency.
Wrong (C): No sampling technique can eliminate sampling risk entirely — only a 100% examination of the population achieves that.
Wrong (D): Stratification enables targeted testing across all strata, not exclusively testing of the largest items.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 304;

-- Q305: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit sampling because items are being selected from a defined population","Not audit sampling because it is a complete examination of the subpopulation","An analytical procedure applied to the highest-value receivable accounts","A violation of the professional standards governing audit sampling"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.05, audit sampling involves applying procedures to less than 100% of items in a population. Testing all items in a stratum constitutes complete examination of that subpopulation, not sampling. AU-C 530.A4 notes auditors commonly combine complete examination of top strata with sampling of remaining strata.
Wrong (A): Because 100% of the stratum is tested, no sampling is involved — sampling requires testing less than the full population.
Wrong (C): Testing all items above a threshold is a substantive detail test, not an analytical procedure that uses relationships among data.
Wrong (D): Testing all high-value items is an accepted audit strategy specifically contemplated by AU-C 530, not a violation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 305;

-- Q307: Audit Sampling [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determine the appropriate sample size for the MUS test of receivables","Measure the statistical reliability of the monetary-unit sampling method","Calculate the tolerable deviation rate for the population being tested","Project the misstatement found in a sampled item to the sampling interval"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 530.A24, the tainting percentage in MUS projects misstatements from sampled items to the population. It is calculated as (Book Value minus Audit Value) divided by Book Value, then multiplied by the sampling interval to compute the projected misstatement for that item.
Wrong (A): Sample size is determined before testing begins using tolerable misstatement and expected error, not by the tainting percentage.
Wrong (B): The tainting percentage measures the proportional error in a specific item, not the overall reliability of the sampling method.
Wrong (C): Tolerable deviation rate applies to attributes sampling for tests of controls, not to monetary-unit sampling for substantive testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 307;

-- Q309: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The population is acceptable as stated and no further action is required","The sample was too large and should have been reduced before testing","The population may be materially misstated and additional procedures are needed","Sampling risk has been eliminated through the monetary-unit sampling technique"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530.14, if the projected misstatement ($185,000) plus the allowance for sampling risk ($90,000) exceeds tolerable misstatement ($250,000), the auditor cannot conclude the population is free from material misstatement. The auditor should consider expanding the sample, performing alternative procedures, or requesting management to investigate.
Wrong (A): The sampling results indicate a risk of material misstatement that must be resolved before accepting the population.
Wrong (B): A larger sample would not cause the excess — the issue is that detected misstatements project to a high population error.
Wrong (D): No sampling technique eliminates sampling risk; it exists whenever less than 100% of the population is tested.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 309;

-- Q313: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It requires specialized computer software to implement the selection process","A single block of consecutive items may not represent the entire population","It is substantially more expensive to execute than random selection methods","It can only be applied to tests of controls, not substantive procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.A11, block selection — choosing a contiguous set of items such as all transactions in a single month — is discouraged because a single block may not represent the full population across the entire period. Conditions in the selected block may differ from other periods.
Wrong (A): Block selection does not require computer software — it is a manual method of selecting consecutive items from a population.
Wrong (C): Block selection is typically simpler and less costly than random selection, not more expensive.
Wrong (D): Block selection can technically be used for any type of test, but its representativeness limitation discourages its use regardless of purpose.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 313;

-- Q316: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s estimate without performing any additional audit inquiry","Review the aging schedule, evaluate historical rates, and assess specific accounts","Confirm all individual receivable balances directly with the entity''s customers","Calculate the allowance as a fixed percentage applied to total receivable balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 540.13, testing an accounting estimate requires evaluating the data and assumptions underlying it. For the allowance for doubtful accounts, this means reviewing the aging schedule, analyzing historical collection experience, and assessing specific customer circumstances.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s estimate without inquiry violates AU-C 540''s requirement to evaluate the reasonableness of significant estimates.
Wrong (C): Confirming receivable balances tests existence and accuracy of receivables, not the reasonableness of the allowance estimate.
Wrong (D): A fixed percentage is arbitrary and ignores account-specific risk factors required for a reasonable estimate under GAAP.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 316;

-- Q317: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate management''s plans to mitigate the going concern conditions","Immediately issue a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements","Withdraw from the engagement due to the substantial doubt identified","Issue a qualified opinion citing the recurring operating losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 570.12, when the auditor identifies conditions raising substantial doubt about going concern, the auditor must evaluate management''s plans to mitigate the conditions. Plans may include asset dispositions, debt restructuring, or cost reductions, and the auditor assesses whether they are feasible and likely effective.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer is not automatically issued upon identifying going concern indicators — evaluation of management''s plans is required first.
Wrong (C): Withdrawing from the engagement is not the appropriate response to going concern uncertainty; the auditor must first evaluate management''s plans.
Wrong (D): A qualified opinion is not issued solely because going concern indicators exist — the auditor must first complete the evaluation process under AU-C 570.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 317;

-- Q318: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["One year from the date of the entity''s balance sheet","One year from when the financial statements are issued or available to be issued","Two years from the date of the entity''s balance sheet","Six months from the date the auditor''s report is released"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 570.03, the going concern evaluation covers a reasonable period defined as one year from the date the financial statements are issued or available to be issued. This is a critical distinction from the balance sheet date.
Wrong (A): Measuring from the balance sheet date would shorten the evaluation period and does not match the standard''s requirement.
Wrong (C): Two years exceeds the reasonable period specified by AU-C 570 for going concern evaluation.
Wrong (D): Six months is too short and does not align with the one-year standard under AU-C 570.03.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 318;

-- Q319: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an adverse opinion due to the unresolved going concern doubt","Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph added","Issue a disclaimer of opinion citing inability to resolve the uncertainty","Withdraw from the engagement because substantial doubt remains unresolved"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 570.20 and AU-C 706.06, when substantial doubt about going concern exists and the entity provides adequate disclosure, the auditor issues an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph drawing attention to the going concern disclosure.
Wrong (A): An adverse opinion is issued for material misstatements, not for adequately disclosed going concern uncertainty.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer applies to scope limitations where sufficient evidence cannot be obtained, not to going concern situations with adequate evidence.
Wrong (D): Withdrawal is not required when sufficient evidence has been obtained and disclosures are adequate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 319;

-- Q327: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A matter that automatically results in a modified audit opinion","A matter communicated to the audit committee involving especially challenging judgment","A material weakness identified in internal controls over financial reporting","A deficiency that requires the auditor to issue a disclaimer of opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under PCAOB AS 3101.11, a CAM must meet three criteria: communicated or required to be communicated to the audit committee, related to material accounts or disclosures, and involved especially challenging, subjective, or complex auditor judgment.
Wrong (A): A CAM does not change the type of opinion issued — it is additional communication, not a basis for modification.
Wrong (C): A material weakness in ICFR is a separate reporting concept under AS 2201, distinct from the CAM requirements.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer results from scope limitations, not from the identification of critical audit matters.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 327;

-- Q329: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A material misstatement has been identified in the financial statements","The auditor wants to highlight a matter appropriately disclosed in the statements","The auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence for an account","The client has decided to change its external accounting firm going forward"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 706.06, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph draws attention to a matter appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements that is fundamental to users'' understanding. Common situations include going concern, significant related-party transactions, and adoption of new standards.
Wrong (A): A material misstatement results in a modified opinion (qualified or adverse), not an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (C): Inability to obtain sufficient evidence is a scope limitation leading to a qualified opinion or disclaimer, not an emphasis paragraph.
Wrong (D): A change in accounting firm is not a matter disclosed in the financial statements requiring emphasis-of-matter treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 329;

-- Q332: Audit Reports [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An adverse opinion on internal controls and a qualified opinion on the financial statements","A disclaimer of opinion on both internal controls and the financial statements","Automatic withdrawal from the engagement due to the severity of the weakness","An adverse ICFR opinion, with the financial statement opinion assessed separately"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under PCAOB AS 2201.90, a material weakness requires an adverse opinion on ICFR. However, the financial statement opinion is determined separately based on whether the statements are materially misstated. An adverse ICFR opinion can coexist with an unmodified financial statement opinion if misstatements were detected and corrected.
Wrong (A): A material weakness does not automatically result in a qualified financial statement opinion — the two opinions are determined independently.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer applies to scope limitations, not to identified material weaknesses where sufficient evidence has been obtained.
Wrong (C): Withdrawal is not required when a material weakness is found — the auditor completes the engagement and issues appropriate opinions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 332;

-- Q335: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The date of the entity''s balance sheet being reported upon","The date the engagement letter was signed by both parties","The date sufficient appropriate audit evidence was obtained to support the opinion","Sixty days after the balance sheet date of the audited statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 700.41, the report date should be no earlier than the date the auditor obtained sufficient appropriate audit evidence to support the opinion, including evidence regarding subsequent events through that date.
Wrong (A): The balance sheet date precedes completion of audit work and does not reflect when sufficient evidence was obtained.
Wrong (B): The engagement letter date marks when the engagement begins, not when sufficient evidence is gathered to support the opinion.
Wrong (D): There is no 60-day rule for the audit report date — the date is tied to obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 335;

-- Q338: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A preparation engagement requires the accountant to maintain independence","A preparation engagement requires the accountant to perform analytical procedures","A preparation engagement provides limited assurance on the financial statements","No report is issued in a preparation — only a legend on each page is required"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 70.05, a preparation engagement does not result in a report. Instead, each page of the financial statements must include a legend stating that no assurance is provided. A compilation under AR-C 80.20 requires a written compilation report.
Wrong (A): Neither a preparation nor a compilation engagement requires the accountant to be independent, though AR-C 80.24 requires independence disclosure in compilations.
Wrong (B): Analytical procedures are a review engagement requirement under AR-C 90, not a preparation engagement requirement.
Wrong (C): A preparation engagement provides no assurance whatsoever — limited assurance is the level provided by a review engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 338;

-- Q341: Review and Compilation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Convert the review engagement to a full audit of the financial statements","Issue a qualified review conclusion noting the potential material misstatement","Withdraw from the engagement immediately without performing further work","Perform additional procedures to determine whether material modification is needed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 90.60–.62, if the accountant becomes aware of information indicating the financial statements may be materially misstated during a review, the accountant should perform additional procedures sufficient to resolve the matter. These procedures remain within the scope of a review.
Wrong (A): Converting to an audit is not required — additional review-level procedures such as revised inquiries or targeted analytics may be sufficient.
Wrong (B): A qualified review conclusion is not issued before first performing additional procedures to determine whether modification is actually needed.
Wrong (C): Immediate withdrawal is not required when additional review procedures may resolve the concern.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 341;

-- Q342: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Has obtained reasonable assurance about the accuracy of the financial statements","Has not audited or reviewed the statements and expresses no opinion or conclusion","Provides limited assurance that no material modifications are needed to the statements","Has tested the entity''s internal controls and found them to be effective"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 80.21, the compilation report explicitly states that the accountant has not audited or reviewed the financial statements and accordingly does not express an opinion or a conclusion on them.
Wrong (A): Reasonable assurance is the level provided by an audit engagement, not a compilation.
Wrong (C): Limited assurance is provided in a review engagement under AR-C 90, not in a compilation.
Wrong (D): A compilation does not involve testing internal controls — that is an audit procedure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 342;

-- Q343: Review and Compilation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Never permitted under any circumstances regardless of the engagement type","Permitted in a compilation if the omission is disclosed and not intended to mislead","Permitted in both compilation and review engagements when the client requests it","Only permitted when the client is a governmental entity subject to special rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 80.23, SSARS allows management to omit substantially all required disclosures in a compilation if the omission is clearly indicated in the report and is not undertaken with the intent to mislead financial statement users.
Wrong (A): SSARS specifically permits disclosure omission in compilations under the conditions described, so a blanket prohibition is incorrect.
Wrong (C): Review engagements under AR-C 90 require complete GAAP disclosures — the omission option applies only to compilations.
Wrong (D): The compilation disclosure omission option is available to all entities, not restricted to governmental entities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 343;

-- Q344: Review and Compilation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the reason for the change and determine whether it is appropriate","Automatically agree to the change in order to accommodate the client''s budget","Refuse the request because engagement downgrades are always prohibited by standards","Issue the original audit report regardless of the client''s change request"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.67–.69, when a client requests a change from an audit to a review, the accountant must evaluate the reason. AR-C 90.68 identifies legitimate reasons such as changes in lender requirements. AR-C 90.69 states unacceptable reasons include preventing discovery of misstatements.
Wrong (B): Automatically accepting without evaluating the reason fails to meet professional standards requiring assessment of the change''s appropriateness.
Wrong (C): Engagement downgrades are not categorically prohibited — they are permitted when the reason is legitimate and appropriate.
Wrong (D): Issuing an audit report when the engagement has been appropriately changed would not reflect the actual scope of work performed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 344;

-- Q350: Attestation Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GAAP and IFRS financial reporting frameworks for accuracy of financial data","Trust Services Criteria: security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy","The COSO Internal Control Framework as the exclusive evaluation standard","SEC reporting and disclosure requirements applicable to public registrants"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 205 and SSAE 18, a SOC 2 report evaluates controls based on the Trust Services Criteria (TSC): security (required for all SOC 2 reports), availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy. The service organization selects which additional criteria beyond security are relevant.
Wrong (A): GAAP and IFRS are financial reporting frameworks used for preparing financial statements, not criteria for SOC 2 engagements.
Wrong (C): While COSO principles inform the TSC, SOC 2 specifically uses the Trust Services Criteria, not the COSO framework directly.
Wrong (D): SEC reporting requirements apply to public company filings, not to SOC 2 service organization control evaluations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 350;

-- Q353: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["File a federal tax return with the IRS reporting the grant expenditures","Have a single audit or program-specific audit of their federal award programs","Obtain a SOC 1 Type 2 report on internal controls over grant processing","Submit to a PCAOB inspection of their financial reporting processes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Single Audit Act and Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.501), entities expending $750,000 or more of federal awards in a fiscal year must have a single audit or program-specific audit under GAGAS. The auditor tests compliance with requirements applicable to major federal programs.
Wrong (A): Filing a tax return with the IRS is unrelated to the Single Audit Act requirement, which governs audit oversight of federal award expenditures.
Wrong (C): A SOC 1 report evaluates controls at service organizations, not federal award compliance at recipient entities.
Wrong (D): PCAOB inspections apply to firms auditing public companies, not to entities receiving federal grants.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 353;

-- Q354: Attestation Engagements [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the financial statements, consistent with standard GAAS audit requirements","Internal control and compliance with laws and regulations, in addition to the statements","Only compliance with specific grant requirements applicable to the entity","The entity''s budget-to-actual performance comparison for the fiscal year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GAGAS Chapter 6, Government Auditing Standards impose reporting requirements beyond GAAS. The auditor must report on internal control over financial reporting and compliance with laws, regulations, contracts, and grant agreements that could directly and materially affect the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Reporting only on financial statements omits the additional GAGAS requirements for internal control and compliance reporting.
Wrong (C): Yellow Book reporting covers both internal control and broad legal compliance, not only grant-specific requirements.
Wrong (D): Budget-to-actual performance is a supplementary schedule, not a required Yellow Book reporting element.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 354;

-- Q357: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Security, which is required as common criteria for every SOC 2 engagement","Availability, which addresses whether the system is operational and accessible","Financial reporting accuracy, which evaluates correctness of financial data","Confidentiality, which protects information designated as confidential by the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): The five Trust Services Criteria are security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy. Financial reporting accuracy is not a TSC category — financial reporting controls are addressed by SOC 1 reports under the SSAE framework, not SOC 2.
Wrong (A): Security is the common criteria required for all SOC 2 engagements and is a valid Trust Services Criterion.
Wrong (B): Availability is one of the five Trust Services Criteria, addressing whether the system is operational as committed.
Wrong (D): Confidentiality is one of the five Trust Services Criteria, protecting information designated as confidential.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 357;

-- Q364: Quality Management [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["PCAOB inspections apply to public company auditors; peer review covers private company auditors","Peer review is substantially more rigorous than PCAOB inspections of audit quality","PCAOB inspections are voluntary for firms that audit publicly traded companies","Peer review applies exclusively to tax preparation practices, not audit engagements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The PCAOB conducts inspections of firms that audit public companies (issuers) under the Sarbanes-Oxley Act. AICPA peer review applies to firms performing audits, reviews, and compilations of private companies (non-issuers). Firms auditing both are subject to both oversight mechanisms.
Wrong (B): PCAOB inspections and peer review serve different populations but peer review is not more rigorous — PCAOB inspections are generally considered more intensive.
Wrong (C): PCAOB inspections are mandatory for registered firms that audit issuers, not voluntary.
Wrong (D): Peer review covers audit, review, and compilation engagements for non-issuers, not exclusively tax practices.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 364;
